node_modules
# `.next*`, not `.next` — the glob is the whole point. `distDir` is env-configurable
# (next.config.ts), and the suites use it: `.next-e2e` for the e2e run and
# `.next-e2e-prod` for the production one. A bare `.next` matches none of those, so a
# machine that had run the e2e was shipping 1.1GB of stale build output into every image
# build. Measured at "transferring context: 1.11GB" before this line changed.
.next*
.git
.gitignore
.env*
!.env.example
# `e2e*`, for the same reason as `.next*` above and it is the same bug twice. `e2e` alone
# does not match `e2e-prod`, so the production suite was copied into the image WITHOUT the
# `e2e/` helpers it imports — and `next build` type-checks the whole project, so the image
# build died on `Cannot find module '../e2e/_login'`. A test directory failing a production
# build is a confusing enough error to be worth this comment.
e2e*
coverage
test-results
# `playwright*` covers the report directory AND the two root configs, which is the third
# instance of this same bug: both import from `./e2e/`, which is excluded above, and
# `next build` type-checks the whole project — so a Playwright config sitting at the repo
# root failed the production image build. Test infrastructure has no business in the image
# regardless of whether it happens to compile.
playwright*
.claude
.code-review-graph
docker
docs
README.md
*.log
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
